WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:06.680
You think you've explored medicine. In reality, you're standing in the parking lot of Disney World bragging about how fun

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:11.920
the pavement is. You've taken pictures of the asphalt. You've written dissertations about the parking stripes.

3
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:21.380
You've never gone inside. After a century of pharmacology, most beneficial effects of molecules on diseases remain unexplored.

4
00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:29.760
Not because biology is constrained. Because clinical trials are slow, expensive, and run by people who think "urgency" is

5
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:36.280
a type of perfume. Here's the math. It's painful but brief, like a flu shot administered by someone who hates you.

6
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:43.020
Your Tiny Sandbox. There are twenty thousand prescription products approved in the U.S.

7
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.780
It sounds impressive until you realize most are just "Tylenol, but blue."

8
00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:52.640
These products contain only one thousand six hundred and fifty unique compounds.

9
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.640
That is fewer ingredients than a decent French bakery uses.

10
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:03.160
Seven thousand five hundred investigational compounds have passed Phase One globally.

11
00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.940
Progress! The F.D.A. GRAS list contains six hundred and thirty-five substances.

12
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.500
These are things you could eat right now without dying. Low bar.

13
00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:21.540
Still useful. Total unique compounds humanity knows are safe-ish: nine thousand five hundred compounds.

14
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:27.240
That's it. That's your entire medicine cabinet after five millennia.

15
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:36.380
The Target List (Ways Your Body Breaks). The I.C.D. ten lists fourteen thousand codes for ways your meat can malfunction.

16
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.340
It is humanity's most comprehensive to-do list, and you're ignoring it.

17
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:47.120
After consolidation, there are one thousand trial-relevant diseases worth actually fixing.

18
00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:53.240
The Math You're Ignoring. Take your nine thousand five hundred safe compounds.

19
00:01:53.600 --> 00:02:01.020
Multiply by one thousand diseases. This gives you the "Combinatorial Space," which is fancy talk for "stuff you could test

20
00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:08.300
tomorrow if you weren't busy filling out forms." Nine thousand five hundred compounds times one thousand diseases equals

21
00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:13.160
nine million five hundred thousand plausible drug-condition combinations.

22
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:21.560
What You've Actually Tested. There are one thousand seven hundred and fifty unique approved drug-disease pairings.

23
00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:29.160
That is your entire medical canon. Thirty percent of drugs gain at least one new indication after initial approval.

24
00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:36.380
The rest never get a second date. Let's be generous and say you tested ten times as many things that failed.

25
00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:43.580
(You did. You learned nothing from them.) Total tested: thirty-two thousand five hundred relationships.

26
00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:51.980
The Scorecard. Thirty-two thousand five hundred relationships tested out of nine million five hundred thousand possibilities

27
00:02:52.660 --> 00:03:02.400
equals zero point three four two percent. Humanity has explored less than one percent of the theoretically testable drug-disease

28
00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.620
space. You are the detective who searched one drawer and declared the case cold.

29
00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:13.480
It's like reading the first page of War and Peace and writing a book report called "It's About a Party."

30
00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:20.640
Technically accurate. Cosmically insufficient. Is The Untested Stuff Useful?

31
00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:26.800
You might argue, "Maybe the other 99 percent is useless!". Biology would like a word.

32
00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:35.080
Undrugged Targets. Mapping three hundred and fifty thousand plus clinical trials showed that only twelve percent of the

33
00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:42.400
human interactome has ever been targeted by drugs. You're ignoring eighty-eight percent of your own biology.

34
00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:47.320
It's like having a one hundred-room mansion and living exclusively in the bathroom.

35
00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:53.060
Polypharmacology. Drugs are messy. They hit multiple targets.

36
00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:57.760
F.D.A.-approved drugs often bind to things you didn't intend.

37
00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:03.700
This means "side effects" are frequently "cures for something else" that nobody bothered to notice.

38
00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:08.900
Every headache pill might cure Alzheimer's. You won't know until you check.

39
00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:16.060
You're not checking. Network Overlap. Diseases cluster on shared biological networks.

40
00:04:16.899 --> 00:04:24.020
Arthritis and Alzheimer's share biological pathways. The cure for your grandmother's dementia might be sitting in your grandfather's

41
00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:30.660
medicine cabinet. But you'll never find out, because testing it would require paperwork that takes longer than Grandma has

42
00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:38.340
left. The Universe is Big. You Are Small. So far we only counted molecules humanity already has.

43
00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:43.890
The stuff in your cupboards. If you look at what's chemically possible, it gets embarrassing.

44
00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:52.300
Chemical Space. Estimated ten to the twenty-third to ten to the sixtieth power drug-like molecules.

45
00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:57.580
That's more candidates than there are atoms in your body. Or stars in the galaxy.

46
00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:03.360
Or excuses from your regulatory agencies. Virtual Libraries.

47
00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:07.580
ZINC twenty-two contains tens of billions of purchasable virtual molecules.

48
00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:17.020
You could order them online. You haven't. Synthesized. You have made less than ten to the negative fortieth power of possible

49
00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:25.480
molecules. Tested divided by possible is approximately ten to the negative twentieth to ten to the negative fiftieth power.

50
00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:34.240
Effectively zero. You haven't even started. This is like exploring Earth by looking at one grain of sand on one beach in

51
00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.160
New Jersey, then declaring you've "seen the world." You haven't.

52
00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:43.300
You've seen a grain of sand. In New Jersey. Why You Are Still Sick.

53
00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:49.440
It's not because science is hard. It's because you've built a system that is allergic to answers.

54
00:05:50.400 --> 00:06:00.560
Trials Cost Too Much. A typical Phase Two to Three randomized controlled trial, or R.C.T., costs thirty to one hundred million

55
00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:04.400
dollars (Median cost per patient forty-one thousand dollars).

56
00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:10.200
At that price, you only test "sure things." Safe bets. Boring bets.

57
00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:19.160
Bets that don't cure cancer. Clinical trials are a velvet-rope nightclub where the bouncer is a regulatory agency and the

58
00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:28.640
cover charge is a hundred million dollars. Herd Mentality. Trials overwhelmingly test the same few biological targets due

59
00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:35.140
to preferential attachment dynamics. Everyone studies what everyone else studies because that's how you get grants.

60
00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:42.290
It's like if every explorer only went to France because France already had good reviews on TripAdvisor.

61
00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:47.540
Meanwhile, undiscovered continents full of cures sit there, undiscovered.

62
00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:55.800
No Profit: Most molecules have no patent. No patent equals no one hundred million dollar trial which equals no approval.

63
00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:04.160
The cure for cancer might be aspirin plus vitamin D. Nobody will ever test it because you can't patent aspirin plus vitamin

64
00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:08.660
D. The molecule that saves your life has to make money first.

65
00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:19.640
If it can't, it won't. You die. Quarterly earnings go up. Neglect: If you have a rare disease with no market, you don't

66
00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:28.020
get a drug. Your cells malfunction in an unprofitable way. The invisible hand of the market has weighed your life against

67
00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:32.400
quarterly earnings and found you wanting. Sorry about your death.

68
00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.760
It was a business decision. How Long to Explore Everything?

69
00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:45.020
At the current pace of three thousand three hundred trials per year, how long would it take to systematically test therapeutic

70
00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:49.860
possibilities? Tier 1: Single Compounds (Most Conservative).

71
00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:59.720
Testing every known safe compound against every major disease: Nine point five million combinations divided by three thousand

72
00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:04.880
three hundred trials per year equals two thousand eight hundred and eighty years.

73
00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:10.980
That's almost three thousand years to explore compounds we already know are safe.

74
00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:14.520
By that math, we won't finish until the year five thousand.

75
00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:23.760
Tier 2: Combination Therapies (Still Defensible). Modern medicine increasingly relies on multi-drug cocktails.

76
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:30.280
Oncology, H.I.V., cardiology, psychiatry: all routinely use drug combinations.

77
00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:36.260
So the real question isn't "does this one drug work?" It's "which combinations work?"

78
00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:45.940
Testing pairwise combinations of safe compounds: Forty-five point one billion combinations divided by three thousand three

79
00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:50.940
hundred trials per year equals thirteen point seven million years.

80
00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:57.910
That's longer than Homo sapiens has existed as a species. And that's using only compounds with established safety data,

81
00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:02.920
testing only pairs. Not triplets. God help you if you want to try triplets.

82
00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:13.500
Tier 3: The Full Chemical Universe (Illustrative). Beyond known compounds lies the chemical space of ten to the twenty-third

83
00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:21.920
power to ten to the sixtieth power drug-like molecules. At current trial capacity, exploring even the synthesizable fraction

84
00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:29.610
would take longer than the universe has existed. The universe is thirteen point eight billion years old.

85
00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:35.460
You need more time than that. To test pills. Pills that already exist.

86
00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:40.690
We haven't even started. "But Won't We Run Out of Easy Discoveries?".

87
00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:47.460
Some will argue "diminishing returns": the more we search, the harder each new discovery becomes.

88
00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:52.860
This assumes we've already picked the low-hanging fruit. We haven't picked any fruit.

89
00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:56.170
We're still in the parking lot arguing about whether fruit exists.

90
00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:02.200
Diminishing returns only kicks in after you've explored a significant fraction of the space.

91
00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:10.240
At less than one percent explored, every trial teaches you something new about human biology, improving your ability to

92
00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:14.680
predict what works next. Learning compounds. Depletion is negligible.

93
00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:20.560
You are nowhere near the bottom of the barrel because you have not yet located the barrel.

94
00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:28.900
The math: even with weak learning, diminishing returns only matters after exploring roughly thirty-seven percent of therapeutic

95
00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:36.040
space. For combination therapies, that crossover is roughly seven million years away at current pace.

96
00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:40.360
You cannot have diminishing returns when you've barely started looking.

97
00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:50.320
See the formal analysis for the equations. The Fix. The Oxford RECOVERY Trial (COVID nineteen) proved that large-scale pragmatic

98
00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:56.940
trials can run for approximately five hundred dollars and deliver results in less than one hundred days.

99
00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:02.920
Five hundred dollars versus forty-one thousand dollars. Read those numbers again.

100
00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:12.080
One of them is the reason people are dying. With a decentralized framework for drug assessment, trial capacity increases

101
00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:20.980
by twelve point three times: Nine point five million combinations divided by forty thousand seven hundred trials per year

102
00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:29.180
equals two hundred thirty-four years. Still a long time, but at least it's a number with fewer digits than a phone book.

103
00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:36.360
And the first few decades would capture the highest-value discoveries (the low-hanging fruit that current trial scarcity

104
00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:40.100
forces you to ignore while people die in the waiting room).

105
00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:50.880
More importantly, D.F.D.A. (a decentralized F.D.A.) builds permanent infrastructure for systematic therapeutic exploration.

106
00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:58.960
The goal isn't to "finish." It's to stop leaving cures on the table while you form subcommittees to discuss whether to form

107
00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:04.300
committees to review the proposal to consider them. The Bottom Line.

108
00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:12.740
Humanity has empirically tested less than one percent of the drug-disease relationships that can be tested using existing

109
00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:22.480
safe compounds. If you include the larger chemical universe, humanity has explored roughly one-quadrillionth to one-quintillionth

110
00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:28.600
of what is possible. There are numbers so small that mathematicians get depressed looking at them.

111
00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:36.120
These are those numbers. The overwhelming majority of therapeutic potential remains untouched.

112
00:12:36.140 --> 00:12:41.060
Not because cures are impossible. Because trials are too expensive and too slow.

113
00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:49.460
A decentralized, automated pragmatic trial system is the only route to systematically explore the remaining ninety-nine

114
00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:56.580
point nine nine nine percent of medical space. You're not in the parking lot because there's nothing inside Disney World.

115
00:12:57.440 --> 00:13:05.000
You're in the parking lot because the entrance fee is one hundred million dollars and requires seventeen years of paperwork.

116
00:13:05.740 --> 00:13:09.140
People are dying in the parking lot. The rides are right there.

117
00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:13.840
You can hear the screaming. (Fun screaming. Cure screaming.).

118
00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:16.220
The cures are inside. Open the gate.
